WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.277 --> 00:00:01.110
MAX SALTONSTALL: Hi.

00:00:01.110 --> 00:00:02.940
I'm Max Saltonstall,
and today I'm

00:00:02.940 --> 00:00:04.357
going to walk you
through creating

00:00:04.357 --> 00:00:06.810
your own private certificate
authority with Google Cloud's

00:00:06.810 --> 00:00:09.420
new Certificate
Authority Service.

00:00:09.420 --> 00:00:12.240
This new service can help
you simplify the deployment,

00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:14.880
management, and
security of private CAs

00:00:14.880 --> 00:00:17.110
without managing infrastructure.

00:00:17.110 --> 00:00:18.420
Let's take a look.

00:00:18.420 --> 00:00:20.670
From the console,
go to Security, then

00:00:20.670 --> 00:00:22.380
Certificate Authority Service.

00:00:22.380 --> 00:00:24.840
If you haven't yet,
enable the API now.

00:00:24.840 --> 00:00:31.902
Create a new CA, choose Root
CA, and choose a region.

00:00:31.902 --> 00:00:33.360
You can also see
below that we have

00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:35.580
some premade recommended
configurations

00:00:35.580 --> 00:00:38.350
to help you save time
with CA creation.

00:00:38.350 --> 00:00:40.560
Next, configure the
new CA, filling out

00:00:40.560 --> 00:00:42.660
the necessary fields.

00:00:42.660 --> 00:00:46.470
The ID is the unique identifier
for this resource inside Google

00:00:46.470 --> 00:00:48.750
Cloud and will be used
by other services.

00:00:48.750 --> 00:00:52.454
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:54.310 --> 00:00:55.560
Now we need a key.

00:00:55.560 --> 00:00:57.265
In this case, I've
already created one

00:00:57.265 --> 00:00:58.890
using the Key Management
Service, which

00:00:58.890 --> 00:01:01.870
I add at this step.

00:01:01.870 --> 00:01:04.440
You can do it the same way, or
use a hardware security module

00:01:04.440 --> 00:01:05.550
key.

00:01:05.550 --> 00:01:07.518
As we update the Certificate
Authority Service,

00:01:07.518 --> 00:01:09.060
we'll be adding the
ability to create

00:01:09.060 --> 00:01:10.980
a key from inside this tool.

00:01:10.980 --> 00:01:13.050
Now onto revocation,
for when we need

00:01:13.050 --> 00:01:16.770
to tell clients to no longer
trust this certificate.

00:01:16.770 --> 00:01:20.470
We use certificate revocation
lists to communicate this.

00:01:20.470 --> 00:01:23.340
You can turn off
CRLs with this box.

00:01:23.340 --> 00:01:25.950
Below, you can also decide to
enable authority certificate

00:01:25.950 --> 00:01:28.140
publication that
enables chain validation

00:01:28.140 --> 00:01:31.470
code on a recipient device to
navigate the full certificate

00:01:31.470 --> 00:01:32.970
chain to the root.

00:01:32.970 --> 00:01:35.160
Next, you can add a label
to enable easy grouping

00:01:35.160 --> 00:01:37.950
of your cloud resources.

00:01:37.950 --> 00:01:42.270
One final review to finalize
details before you create.

00:01:42.270 --> 00:01:46.170
If it all looks good,
then let's run with it.

00:01:46.170 --> 00:01:48.420
You'll now see the newly
created certificate authority

00:01:48.420 --> 00:01:50.730
under the CA Manager tab.

00:01:50.730 --> 00:01:51.600
Great.

00:01:51.600 --> 00:01:53.550
We've got our certificate
authority setup.

00:01:53.550 --> 00:01:55.620
Let's request a certificate
for my fictional

00:01:55.620 --> 00:02:00.060
get snacks to joggers on
the go company, Eat and Run.

00:02:00.060 --> 00:02:01.760
Choose the authority
you want to use,

00:02:01.760 --> 00:02:04.450
and click Request Certificate.

00:02:04.450 --> 00:02:05.882
Click on Provide CSR.

00:02:05.882 --> 00:02:07.590
Then add your certificate
signing request

00:02:07.590 --> 00:02:09.000
in the text box.

00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:11.049
I'll use one I
made with OpenSSL.

00:02:11.049 --> 00:02:14.472
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:02:15.450 --> 00:02:17.640
You can see all certificates
issued by your CA

00:02:17.640 --> 00:02:20.610
under Issued Certificates.

00:02:20.610 --> 00:02:23.160
Click on the dots to download
any certificate for inspection

00:02:23.160 --> 00:02:25.440
or use.

00:02:25.440 --> 00:02:26.670
Congratulations!

00:02:26.670 --> 00:02:28.350
You've created a
new CA and gotten

00:02:28.350 --> 00:02:30.000
a new certificate in minutes--

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:32.760
much faster than the days or
weeks it would take to host it

00:02:32.760 --> 00:02:33.940
yourself.

00:02:33.940 --> 00:02:35.490
Check out the link
for more details

00:02:35.490 --> 00:02:36.750
about Certificate Authority.

00:02:36.750 --> 00:02:40.100
[MUSIC PLAYING]

